  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Nomada placida, commonly known as the Placid Nomad Bee, is a small cuckoo bee in the Apidae family (Honey Bees, Bumble Bees, and Allies) native to New England. These parasitic bees lay their eggs in the nests of other bee species, particularly andrena bees, rather than building their own nests.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Small Size and Dark Coloration",
      "description": "Nomada placida is a tiny bee (4-6mm) with a predominantly black body and minimal hair, making it noticeably smaller and less fuzzy than bumble bees.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Smooth Appearance",
      "description": "Unlike fuzzy mining bees, nomad bees have a sleek, polished exoskeleton with sparse body hair.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Active Flight Pattern",
      "description": "These bees are fast, erratic fliers often seen hovering near the entrances of host bee burrows in early spring.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Nomada placida is found in open woodlands, meadows, and gardens throughout New England where its andrena host bees nest in bare ground or sandy soil.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "As a cuckoo bee, Nomada placida is a 'nest parasite' that sneaks into andrena bee burrows to lay its eggs, allowing the host bee to unknowingly raise the nomad bee's offspring instead of its own.",
